  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Friday:\n\nAn upper level low continues to churn over the northern Ontario \nkeeping the region in northwest flow. At the surface we have high \npressure that expands across Manitoba and through the Midwest. This \ncombination will lead to mostly sunny skies with some diurnal \ncumulus building in during the afternoon. Overall, quiet weather \nconditions today with highs climbing into the 70s. Overnight, rapid \nradiational cooling may lead to areas of dense fog developing. If \nfog does develop it will quickly burn off after sunrise. Friday is \nalso shaping up to be an inactive day. Surface high pressure begins \nto depart the region and we will see increasing cloud coverage \nmoving in from the west late in the day. Expect highs to increase a \nfew degrees from the previous day with a few spots getting into the \nlow 80s. \n\nWeekend:\n\nThe weather pattern turns active again for the weekend. Cross \nbarrier flow over the Rockies will lead to a surface low pressure \ndeveloping in the Northern Plains early Saturday morning. An \nincreasing low level jet out ahead of this system may lead to some \nrain chances beginning early Saturday morning. The 12Z suite of \ndeterministic guidance still shows a high amount of variability in \nthe QPF fields but leveraging the ensembles shows the best chances \nwill be across our southern half of the CWA where the NBM is \nhighlighting PoPs of 40-60%. Both the low pressure and low \nlevel jet weaken through the day, precipitation chances will \npersist but be on the downward trend. \n\nSunday looks to give us another dose of moisture. The GFS IVT would \nsuggest that we should see an increase in available PWATs with \ncontributions from the southern Pacific and possibly Baja. Early \nsignals in the GEFs indicate this would put us near the 90th \npercentile of climatology. As this moisture overspreads the \nNorthland we will have a few synoptic players to help generate \nsome precipitation. However, placement and timing of key \nfeatures remain in flux. The big picture looks to include a pair\nof surface lows moving over the Central and Northern Plains. \nThe northernmost low may have a warm front draped across \nnorthern MN and promote some better rain chances. Strong to \nsevere storm development remains questionable. Mid level flow \nwill be strong enough to support some deep layer shear. There \nwill also be seem steep mid level lapse rates in play leading to\nsome favorable ingredients for large hail. \n\nEarly next week:\n\nThe weather pattern remains active as we head into the early \nportions of the work week. An upper level low moving across the \nCanadian Prairies will promote continued moisture advection across \nthe area. Weather models depict several instances of impulses \nembedded in the flow aloft pushing through the region. There aren't \nany strong signals to really hone in on at this juncture but the \nchances to put more moisture into the soil will be most welcome, \nespecially since portions of the CWA saw an expansion of the extreme \ndrought status.",
